<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đurađ II Balšić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1403">1403</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Đurađ II Stracimirović · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšić noble family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đurađ II Balšić, son of Stracimir and so also called Stracimirović, was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> from 1385 to 1403. Caught between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, whose overlordship he accepted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, he handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a> and other towns to Venice in 1396. He fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> at the Battle of Tripolje in 1402 and died of his wounds the following year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đurađ II was the son of Stracimir, one of the three Balšić brothers who came to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> in the 1360s, and of Milica Mrnjavčević, daughter of King Vukašin Mrnjavčević. When his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a> was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Battle of Savra</a> on 18 September 1385, Đurađ inherited parts of Zeta and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a>, Drivast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a>, under the family's rule of seniority, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a> his seat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His lands soon shrank. Pal Dukagjini took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> area, the Jonima family seceded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> took Peć and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a>, and in Upper Zeta Radič Crnojević opposed him, leaving Đurađ a strip of land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a> and the Adriatic Sea. In 1386 he recognised Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a> as his sovereign and married his daughter Jelena Lazarević, joining Lazar and Vuk Branković in a family alliance over the Serbian realm. Yet he also kept ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and his troops fought beside Lala Şâhin Paşa at the Battle of Bileća in 1388.</p><p>After Tvrtko I died in 1391, Đurađ seized Kotor. His cousin Konstantin entered Ottoman service, and Đurađ turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Late in 1392 Yiğit Bey captured him in battle, and to win his release he surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a>, Drivast and Sveti Srđ and agreed to pay tribute. In October 1395 he retook them and in April 1396 handed Scutari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a> with its islands and Sveti Srđ to Venetian administration for 1,000 ducats a year.</p><p>In May 1396 he defeated the Crnojević brothers and killed Radič. The Hungarian King Sigismund, returning from his defeat at Nicopolis, made him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a>. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> worsened, and in early 1401 Venice stopped paying the tribute; unable to get Ottoman help, Đurađ accepted its demands, and Venice resumed the payment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late summer 1402 his brother-in-law Despot Stefan Lazarević, returning from the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Angora</a>, stayed at his court, and Đurađ raised an army that helped him win at Tripolje near Gračanica in November. He died of his wounds in April 1403 and was buried in the Church of Saint Catherine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. His seventeen-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-iii">Balša III</a> succeeded him. Đurađ founded the Beška Monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a>, and most historians identify him with the epic hero Banović Strahinja.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đurađ II Balšić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Đurađ II Stracimirović</b> gave Skadar to Venice in 1396 to escape Ottoman pressure.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Principality of Zeta, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.5&amp;year=1393" aria-label="Principality of Zeta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Đurađ II Balšić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4337 3945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4337 3945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.07,19.5&amp;year=1393">What was here in 1393? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
